WEBVTT

00:00.560 --> 00:01.730
Part b.

00:01.730 --> 00:04.650
"Determine the x-coordinate
of the point at which g

00:04.650 --> 00:08.820
has an absolute maximum
on the interval negative 4

00:08.820 --> 00:11.760
is less than or equal to x,
is less than or equal to 3.

00:11.760 --> 00:13.564
And justify your answer."

00:13.564 --> 00:15.480
So let's just think about
it in general terms.

00:15.480 --> 00:17.188
If we just think about
a general function

00:17.188 --> 00:20.300
over an interval, where it
could have an absolute maximum.

00:20.300 --> 00:22.750
So let me draw some
axes over here.

00:22.750 --> 00:25.110
And I'm speaking in
general terms first,

00:25.110 --> 00:27.110
and then we can go back
to our function g, which

00:27.110 --> 00:29.920
is derived from this
function f right over here.

00:29.920 --> 00:32.560
So let's say that these
are my coordinate axes,

00:32.560 --> 00:34.950
and let's say we care
about some interval here.

00:34.950 --> 00:37.520
So let's say this is the
interval that I care about.

00:37.520 --> 00:39.480
A function could look
something like this.

00:43.100 --> 00:46.190
And in this case,
its absolute maximum

00:46.190 --> 00:49.000
is going to occur at the
beginning of the interval.

00:49.000 --> 00:53.840
Or a function could look
something like this.

00:53.840 --> 00:55.430
And then the absolute
maximum could

00:55.430 --> 00:58.070
occur at the end
point of the interval.

00:58.070 --> 01:00.550
Or the other possibility
is that the function

01:00.550 --> 01:06.720
looks something like this.

01:06.720 --> 01:08.580
At which point,
the maximum would

01:08.580 --> 01:10.800
be at this critical point.

01:10.800 --> 01:13.750
And I say critical point, as
opposed to just a point where

01:13.750 --> 01:16.419
the slope is zero, because
it's possible to the functions

01:16.419 --> 01:17.460
not differentiable there.

01:17.460 --> 01:21.819
You could imagine a function
that looks like this,

01:21.819 --> 01:23.610
and maybe wouldn't be
differentiable there.

01:23.610 --> 01:29.895
But that point there still
would be the absolute maximum.

01:29.895 --> 01:31.270
So what we really
just have to do

01:31.270 --> 01:35.510
is evaluate g at the different
endpoints of this interval,

01:35.510 --> 01:38.850
to see how high it gets,
or how large of a value

01:38.850 --> 01:40.800
we get for the g
at the end points.

01:40.800 --> 01:44.330
And then we have to see if g has
any critical points in between.

01:44.330 --> 01:45.770
And then evaluate
it there to see

01:45.770 --> 01:49.290
if that's a candidate
for the global maximum.

01:49.290 --> 01:51.820
So let's just evaluate g
of the different points.

01:51.820 --> 01:55.440
So let's start off, let's
evaluate g at negative 4--

01:55.440 --> 01:58.250
at kind of the lowest
end, or the starting point

01:58.250 --> 01:59.720
of our interval.

01:59.720 --> 02:05.210
So g of negative 4 is
equal to 2 times negative 4

02:05.210 --> 02:12.030
plus the integral from 0
to negative 4 f of t dt.

02:12.030 --> 02:16.010
The first part is very easy, 2
times negative 4 is negative 8.

02:16.010 --> 02:18.990
Let me do it over here so
I have some real estate.

02:18.990 --> 02:21.330
So this is equal to negative 8.

02:21.330 --> 02:27.110
And instead of leaving this
as 0 to negative 4 f of t dt,

02:27.110 --> 02:31.280
let's change the bounds
of integration here.

02:31.280 --> 02:34.910
Especially so that we can get
the lower number as the lower

02:34.910 --> 02:35.580
bound.

02:35.580 --> 02:37.121
And that way, it
becomes a little bit

02:37.121 --> 02:39.190
more natural to think
of it in terms of areas.

02:39.190 --> 02:41.540
So this expression
right here can

02:41.540 --> 02:44.460
be rewritten as the
negative of the integral

02:44.460 --> 02:49.410
between negative
4 and 0 f of t dt.

02:49.410 --> 02:52.310
And now this expression
right over here

02:52.310 --> 02:55.860
is the area under f of
t, or in this case, f

02:55.860 --> 03:02.800
of x-- or the area under f
between negative 4 and 0.

03:02.800 --> 03:06.040
So it's this area
right over here.

03:06.040 --> 03:09.040
And we have to be careful,
because this part over here

03:09.040 --> 03:10.980
is below the x-axis.

03:10.980 --> 03:13.290
So this we would
consider negative area

03:13.290 --> 03:15.380
when we think of it
in integration terms.

03:15.380 --> 03:17.170
And this would be positive area.

03:17.170 --> 03:19.640
So the total area here is
going to be this positive area

03:19.640 --> 03:22.050
minus this area right over here.

03:22.050 --> 03:24.180
So let's think
about what this is.

03:24.180 --> 03:27.570
So this area-- This section
over here we did this in part a,

03:27.570 --> 03:30.540
actually, This section--
this is a quarter circle,

03:30.540 --> 03:33.400
so it's 1/4-- so these
are both quarter circles.

03:33.400 --> 03:41.130
So we could multiply 1/4 times
the area of this entire circle,

03:41.130 --> 03:43.580
if we were to draw the entire
thing all the way around.

03:43.580 --> 03:45.340
It has a radius of 3.

03:45.340 --> 03:49.250
So the area of the entire circle
would be pi times 3 squared

03:49.250 --> 03:51.807
or it would be 9 pi.

03:51.807 --> 03:53.390
And of course we're
going to divide it

03:53.390 --> 03:56.440
by 4-- multiply it by 1/4 to
just get this quarter circle

03:56.440 --> 03:57.700
right over there.

03:57.700 --> 03:59.690
And then this area
right over here,

03:59.690 --> 04:05.780
the area of the entire
circle, we have a radius of 1.

04:05.780 --> 04:08.437
So it's going to be
pi times 1 squared.

04:08.437 --> 04:10.020
So it's going to be
pi, and then we're

04:10.020 --> 04:13.525
going to divide it
by four, because it's

04:13.525 --> 04:15.630
only one fourth of that circle.

04:15.630 --> 04:17.149
And we're going
to subtract that.

04:17.149 --> 04:20.200
So we have negative pi
and we were multiplying it

04:20.200 --> 04:21.860
times 1/4 out here,
because it's just

04:21.860 --> 04:23.750
a quarter circle in either case.

04:23.750 --> 04:26.500
And we're subtracting
it, because the area

04:26.500 --> 04:30.170
is below the x-axis.

04:30.170 --> 04:34.210
And so this simplifies to--
this is equal to 1/4 times

04:34.210 --> 04:37.767
8 pi, which is the
same thing as 2 pi.

04:37.767 --> 04:38.600
Did I do that right?

04:38.600 --> 04:39.850
1/4 times 8 pi.

04:39.850 --> 04:42.930
So this all simplifies to 2 pi.

04:42.930 --> 04:51.670
So g of negative 4 is equal
to negative 8 minus 2 pi.

04:51.670 --> 04:54.010
So clearly it is a
negative number here.

04:54.010 --> 04:56.000
More negative than negative 8.

04:56.000 --> 04:57.680
So let's try the other bounds.

04:57.680 --> 05:02.880
So let's see what
g of positive 3 is.

05:02.880 --> 05:05.510
I'll do it over here so
I have some more space.

05:05.510 --> 05:09.290
So g of positive 3,
when x is equal to 3,

05:09.290 --> 05:12.090
that-- we go back to our
definition-- that is 2 times

05:12.090 --> 05:18.760
3 plus the integral
from 0 to 3 f of t dt.

05:18.760 --> 05:21.220
And this is going to be
equal to 2 times 3 is 6.

05:21.220 --> 05:23.740
And the integral from 0
to 3 f of t dt, that's

05:23.740 --> 05:24.880
this entire area.

05:24.880 --> 05:27.010
So we have positive
area over here.

05:27.010 --> 05:30.220
And then we have an equal
negative area right over here

05:30.220 --> 05:32.160
because it's below the x-axis.

05:32.160 --> 05:34.764
So the integral from 0 to
3 is just going to be 0,

05:34.764 --> 05:36.430
you're going to have
this positive area,

05:36.430 --> 05:38.629
and then this negative
area right over here

05:38.629 --> 05:40.170
is going to completely
cancel it out.

05:40.170 --> 05:42.220
Because it's symmetric
right over here.

05:42.220 --> 05:44.243
So this thing is going
to evaluate to 0.

05:44.243 --> 05:46.430
So g of 3 is 6.

05:46.430 --> 05:49.360
So we already know that
our starting point, g

05:49.360 --> 05:51.930
of negative 4-- that when
x is equal to negative 4--

05:51.930 --> 05:54.825
that is not where g
hits a global maximum.

05:54.825 --> 05:56.200
Because that's a
negative number.

05:56.200 --> 05:57.930
And we already
found the end point,

05:57.930 --> 06:00.040
where g hits a positive value.

06:00.040 --> 06:02.160
So negative 4 is
definitely not a candidate.

06:02.160 --> 06:05.320
x is equal to 3 is still in the
running for the x-coordinate

06:05.320 --> 06:08.461
where g has a global
absolute maximum.

06:08.461 --> 06:09.960
Now what we have
to do is figure out

06:09.960 --> 06:13.130
any critical points
that g has in between.

06:13.130 --> 06:15.530
So points in there where
it's either undifferentiable

06:15.530 --> 06:18.670
or its derivative is equal to 0.

06:18.670 --> 06:20.680
So let's look at
this derivative.

06:20.680 --> 06:24.910
So g prime of x-- we just take
the derivative of this business

06:24.910 --> 06:25.410
up here.

06:25.410 --> 06:27.490
Derivative of 2x is 2.

06:27.490 --> 06:30.580
Derivative of this definition
going from 0 to x of f of t

06:30.580 --> 06:33.640
dt-- we did that in part a, this
is just the fundamental theorem

06:33.640 --> 06:38.270
of calculus-- this
is just going to be

06:38.270 --> 06:43.040
plus f of x right over there.

06:43.040 --> 06:44.860
So it actually
turns out that g is

06:44.860 --> 06:47.190
differentiable over
the entire interval.

06:47.190 --> 06:51.430
You give any x value
over this interval,

06:51.430 --> 06:54.260
we have a value for f of x.
f of x isn't differentiable

06:54.260 --> 06:55.950
everywhere, but
definitely f of x

06:55.950 --> 06:57.980
is defined everywhere,
over the interval.

06:57.980 --> 07:01.120
So you'll get a number here,
and obviously two is just two,

07:01.120 --> 07:03.501
and you add two to it, and
you get the derivative of g

07:03.501 --> 07:04.750
at that point in the interval.

07:04.750 --> 07:07.300
So g is actually differentiable
throughout the interval.

07:07.300 --> 07:08.810
So the only critical
points would

07:08.810 --> 07:12.100
be where this derivative
is equal to 0.

07:12.100 --> 07:14.850
So let's set this
thing equal to 0.

07:14.850 --> 07:17.230
So we want to solve the
equation-- I'll just rewrite it

07:17.230 --> 07:20.140
actually-- So we want to solve
the equation g prime of x

07:20.140 --> 07:25.400
is equal to 0, or 2 plus
f of x is equal to 0.

07:25.400 --> 07:27.150
You can subtract
2 from both sides,

07:27.150 --> 07:30.010
and you get f of x is
equal to negative 2.

07:30.010 --> 07:33.750
So any x that satisfies f
of x is equal to negative 2

07:33.750 --> 07:38.630
is a point where the
derivative of g is equal to 0.

07:38.630 --> 07:43.290
And let's see if f of x is equal
to negative 2 at any point.

07:43.290 --> 07:47.179
So let me draw a line
over here at negative 2.

07:47.179 --> 07:49.720
We have to look at it visually,
because there's only given us

07:49.720 --> 07:52.111
this visual
definition of f of x.

07:52.111 --> 07:54.360
Doesn't equal negative 2,
doesn't equal to negative 2,

07:54.360 --> 07:59.800
only equals negative
2 right over there.

07:59.800 --> 08:02.991
And it looks like we're at about
2 and 1/2, but let's get exact.

08:02.991 --> 08:04.990
Let's actually figure out
the slope of the line,

08:04.990 --> 08:06.820
and figure out what
x value actually

08:06.820 --> 08:09.632
gives f of x equal
to negative 2.

08:09.632 --> 08:11.840
And we could figure out the
slope of this line fairly

08:11.840 --> 08:15.020
visually-- or figure out the
equation of this line fairly

08:15.020 --> 08:17.360
visually, we can
figure out its slope.

08:17.360 --> 08:24.630
If we run 3-- if our
change in x is 3,

08:24.630 --> 08:29.890
then our change in y,
our rise, is negative 6.

08:29.890 --> 08:32.450
Change in y is
equal to negative 6.

08:32.450 --> 08:36.110
Slope is rise over run, or
change in y over change in x.

08:36.110 --> 08:38.992
So negative 6 divided
by 3 is negative 2.

08:38.992 --> 08:40.200
It has a slope of negative 2.

08:40.200 --> 08:42.033
And actually, I could
have done that easier.

08:42.033 --> 08:45.780
Where if we go forward
one, we go down by 2.

08:45.780 --> 08:48.760
So we could have seen that
the slope is negative 2.

08:48.760 --> 08:52.000
So this part of
f of x, we have y

08:52.000 --> 08:55.260
is equal to negative
2x plus-- and then

08:55.260 --> 08:57.200
the y-intercept is
pretty straightforward.

08:57.200 --> 08:59.960
This is at 3-- 1, 2, 3.

08:59.960 --> 09:01.830
Negative 2x plus 3.

09:01.830 --> 09:05.500
So part of f of x
where clearly it

09:05.500 --> 09:08.450
equals negative 2 at some point
of that-- this part of f of x

09:08.450 --> 09:09.890
is defined by this line.

09:09.890 --> 09:12.604
Obviously this part of f of x
is not defined by that line.

09:12.604 --> 09:14.020
But to figure out
the exact value,

09:14.020 --> 09:15.920
we just have to figure
out when this line is

09:15.920 --> 09:18.100
equal to negative 2.

09:18.100 --> 09:26.620
So we have negative 2x plus
3 is equal to negative 2.

09:26.620 --> 09:29.010
And remember, this isn't--
this is what f of x is equal

09:29.010 --> 09:31.089
to, over the interval
that we care about.

09:31.089 --> 09:32.880
If we were talking
about f of x over there,

09:32.880 --> 09:34.879
we wouldn't be able to
put a negative 2x plus 3,

09:34.879 --> 09:37.930
we would have to have some form
of equation for these circles.

09:37.930 --> 09:40.910
But right over here, this is
what f of x is, and now we

09:40.910 --> 09:43.730
can solve this pretty
straightforwardly.

09:43.730 --> 09:48.470
So we can subtract
3 from both sides,

09:48.470 --> 09:51.750
and we get negative 2x
is equal to negative 5.

09:51.750 --> 09:53.670
Divide both sides
by negative 2, you

09:53.670 --> 09:56.780
get x is equal to negative
5 over negative 2,

09:56.780 --> 09:58.352
which is equal to 5/2.

09:58.352 --> 09:59.810
Which is exactly
what we thought it

09:59.810 --> 10:02.160
was when we looked
at it visually.

10:02.160 --> 10:04.160
It looked like we were
at about 2 and 1/2, which

10:04.160 --> 10:06.220
is the same thing as 5/2.

10:06.220 --> 10:08.376
Now we don't know what this is.

10:08.376 --> 10:10.250
We don't know if this
is an inflection point.

10:10.250 --> 10:11.260
Is this a maximum?

10:11.260 --> 10:12.280
Is this a minimum?

10:12.280 --> 10:13.970
So really we just
want to evaluate g

10:13.970 --> 10:17.686
at this point to see if it gets
higher than when we evaluate g

10:17.686 --> 10:18.600
at 3.

10:18.600 --> 10:24.140
So let's evaluate g at 5/2.

10:24.140 --> 10:27.030
So g at 5/2 is going
to be equal to 2 times

10:27.030 --> 10:42.020
15/2 plus the integral
from 0 to 5/2 of f of t dt.

10:42.020 --> 10:45.830
So this first part right over
here, the 2's cancel out.

10:45.830 --> 10:50.020
So this is going
to be equal to 5.

10:50.020 --> 10:53.402
And then plus the
integral from 0 to 5/2.

10:53.402 --> 10:55.110
Now, you might be able
to do it visually,

10:55.110 --> 10:58.210
but we know what the value is
of f of t over this interval,

10:58.210 --> 11:01.200
we already figured out
the equation for it

11:01.200 --> 11:02.740
over this interval.

11:02.740 --> 11:08.780
So it's the integral of
negative 2x plus 3 dt.

11:08.780 --> 11:10.714
And then let's
just evaluate this.

11:10.714 --> 11:11.880
Let me get some real estate.

11:11.880 --> 11:13.463
So this is-- let me
draw a line here.,

11:13.463 --> 11:14.550
so we don't get confused.

11:14.550 --> 11:17.390
So this is going to
be equal to 5 plus

11:17.390 --> 11:18.980
and then I take
the antiderivative.

11:18.980 --> 11:24.653
The antiderivative of negative
2x is negative x squared.

11:27.520 --> 11:30.250
So we have negative x squared.

11:30.250 --> 11:35.020
And the antiderivative of
3 is just going to be 3x.

11:35.020 --> 11:37.830
So plus 3x.

11:37.830 --> 11:41.200
And we're going to
evaluate it from 0 to 5/2.

11:41.200 --> 11:44.060
So this is going to
be equal to 5 plus--

11:44.060 --> 11:46.450
and I'll do all this
stuff right over here.

11:46.450 --> 11:48.380
I'll do this stuff in green.

11:48.380 --> 11:50.200
So when we evaluate
it at 5/2, this

11:50.200 --> 11:53.560
is going to be
negative 5/2 squared.

11:53.560 --> 11:57.410
So it's going to be
negative 25 over 4

11:57.410 --> 12:02.139
plus 3 times 5/2,
which is 15 over 2.

12:02.139 --> 12:03.930
And then from that,
we're going to subtract

12:03.930 --> 12:05.170
this evaluated at 0.

12:05.170 --> 12:10.040
But negative 0 squared
plus 3 times 0 is just 0.

12:10.040 --> 12:12.140
So this is what
it simplifies to.

12:12.140 --> 12:15.550
And so what do we
have right over here?

12:15.550 --> 12:19.320
So let's get our ourselves
a common denominator.

12:19.320 --> 12:22.730
Looks like a common denominator
right over here could be 4

12:22.730 --> 12:25.310
So this is equal to--
5 is the same thing

12:25.310 --> 12:34.220
as 20 over 4 minus 25 over
4 and then plus 30 over 4.

12:34.220 --> 12:38.680
So 20 plus 30 is
50 minus 25 is 25.

12:38.680 --> 12:41.850
So this is equal to 25 over 4.

12:41.850 --> 12:46.270
And 25 over 4 is the
same thing as 6 and 1/4.

12:46.270 --> 12:49.170
So when we evaluate our
function at this critical point,

12:49.170 --> 12:51.540
at this thing where the
slope, or the derivative,

12:51.540 --> 12:54.600
is equal to 0, we
got 6 and 1/4, which

12:54.600 --> 12:58.480
is higher than six, which is
what g was at this end point.

12:58.480 --> 13:01.540
And it's definitely higher
than what g was a negative 4.

13:01.540 --> 13:04.940
So the x-coordinate
of the point at which

13:04.940 --> 13:08.210
g has an absolute maximum,
on the interval negative 4

13:08.210 --> 13:14.433
to three is x is equal to 5/2.
